    why=["I started making games by running Garry's Mod DarkRP servers. New Life rebuilds that game for modern Roblox.", "Roblox servers are matchmade with no staff online, and most players are on phones. So the server rules that staff used to enforce had to become mechanics the game enforces itself, and everything had to work on a touch screen as well as a keyboard and mouse.", "It's also where my loot box research meets a real game. Crates show their odds, the upgrade wheel's slices are the true odds, and a crate's glow tells you its rarity honestly before the reveal."],
    how={'tools': ['Roblox Studio', 'Luau', 'Rojo', 'Git', 'ProfileStore', 'Python'], 'ps': ["Shooting feels instant but can't be cheated. The shooter sees hits straight away, then the server checks fire rate, ammo, range, and line of sight before any damage lands. Every message from a client goes through one rate-limited networking module, trap remotes catch exploit tools, and a movement check pulls back speed hacks and teleports.", "Every item saves how lucky each of its rolls was rather than its final numbers, so its stats are worked out from that luck and the current balance. When I rebalance, every existing item moves with it and keeps its place. Saves are session locked, so items can't be duplicated across servers.", "The 85 guns are balanced by time to kill rather than by hand. Each gun has a target time to kill for its rarity, and its damage is solved from its own fire rate, clip, pellets, and reload, so every gun keeps its real-life feel without one clearly beating the rest.", "The code is organised like Garry's Mod addons, one folder per system, kept in Git and synced into Studio with Rojo. Systems talk through Garry's Mod style hooks, so analytics and the server dashboard plug in without touching the systems they watch, and every money change is tagged with where it came from.", "I built my own tools too: an admin panel modelled on the one I used in Garry's Mod, an owner spawner that makes any item with chosen rolls and grade presets, a photo mode, and test dummies that strafe so slows are easy to see."]},
